## If a nougat re-extraction is needed later

Run on a GPU-equipped host (uicgpu recommended per LUCID small-batch OCR policy):

```bash
# Requires: nougat installed (pip install nougat-ocr), CUDA GPU available.
mkdir -p ~/tmp/nougat_wang2018
nougat --out ~/tmp/nougat_wang2018 \
       --model 0.1.0-base \
       --recompute \
       ~/Dropbox/REPLICATE-PROJECT/LUCID-replications/lucid100-cell-survival-dsb-model-2018/paper.pdf
cp ~/tmp/nougat_wang2018/paper.mmd \
   ~/Dropbox/REPLICATE-PROJECT/LUCID-replications/lucid100-cell-survival-dsb-model-2018/extraction/nougat.mmd
```

Expected value-add over the existing `paper.txt`: cleaner LaTeX math extraction
for eqs (1)-(20), especially the (1 - e^{-x})/x forms and Taylor-limit
expressions in eqs (18)-(20). paper.txt from pdftotext tends to garble
inline math into flat unicode.

## Why not run nougat in this backfill

1. Per instruction: no new heavy compute.
2. Existing `paper.txt` was sufficient for the original replication (all 20
   equations were successfully hand-transcribed into `src/wang2018_model.py`
   from paper.txt + paper.html).
3. Nougat-ocr on a CPU-only session would be prohibitively slow; the correct
   dispatch is uicgpu A100 (per Rick's small-batch OCR policy in TOOLS.md).
